    "body": "<p>This Signature model designs elephant-like infrasound rumbles as low-frequency tones with stated frequency, duration, and amplitude, plus a ground-coupling parameter that estimates how much of the signal travels as a seismic wave. The model computes wavelength from frequency, estimates the distance over which the rumble stays above a stated detection threshold, and schedules rumble exchanges between virtual family groups. No infrasound is emitted and no real elephant is recorded: the model is arithmetic, and any rendered rumble is labelled SIMULATED. Its purpose is teaching why low frequency means long range, and giving sound designers a principled way to build deep, long-distance communication signals.</p><h4>Science proof</h4><p>Science proof: the model encodes the documented physics of infrasound: long wavelengths that diffract around obstacles and suffer relatively low atmospheric absorption, plus ground coupling that carries signals seismically. Those are the same physical reasons real elephant rumbles travel kilometres, and the model states its threshold and loss assumptions so its range estimates can be inspected and challenged.</p><h4>Math proof</h4><p>Math proof: at 12 hertz in air, wavelength = 343 / 12 = 28.58 metres, about 28.6 metres. The demo prints 28.58 m at 12 Hz, 22.87 m at 15 Hz, and 17.15 m at 20 Hz, and the ratio of the 12 Hz wavelength to the 20 Hz wavelength, 28.58 / 17.15 = 1.67.</p><h4>Soundscape layer(s)</h4><p>biophony</p><h4>Source</h4><p>Signature design study for this database, October 2026; elephant infrasound physics as in the public edition</p>",
